"""
STEP 10 - Enrich the ZCTA flood events: storm ID, event type, return period,
          observed / modeled flag
===========================================================================
Adds to every ZCTA event of step 9:

  storm_id            one ID per storm: ZCTA events in neighbouring ZCTAs whose
                      peaks are <= STORM_PEAK_GAP days apart, or that match the
                      same NWS storm episode, get the same storm_id
  event_type          tropical cyclone / heavy rainfall - flash flooding /
                      heavy rainfall - river flooding / heavy rainfall - not reported
  event_type_basis    what the label is based on (HURDAT2, Storm Events, streamflow only)
  coastal_flood_reported, big_river_flooding  extra flags (not in our magnitudes)
  return_period_max   largest return period (years) of the reach peaks in the
                      event (Gumbel fit to the 1980-2022 NWM annual maxima)
  magnitude_modeled   NWM peak / 2-yr flow  (always present)
  magnitude_observed  USGS gauge peak / 2-yr flow, if a usable gauge in the ZCTA
                      flooded during the event
  magnitude_best      observed if available, else modeled
  magnitude_source    "observed (USGS)" / "modeled (NWM)" / "imputed" (not used yet)

Inputs : data/zcta/zcta_events_2018.csv, zcta_reaches.csv, zcta_boundaries_LA.gpkg (step 9)
         data/events/events_nwm_2018.parquet, events_usgs_2018.parquet (step 5)
         data/nwm/reach_thresholds_LA.csv (step 3)
         data/validation/storm_events_LA_2018.csv (step 8)
         data/final/parish_daily_2018.csv (step 7)
         HURDAT2 Atlantic hurricane tracks (downloaded automatically from NHC)
Outputs: data/zcta/zcta_events_2018_enriched.csv
         data/zcta/storms_2018.csv            one row per storm (incl. the list of ZCTAs)
         data/zcta/figures/fig14_storms_2018.png
Usage  : python 10_enrich_zcta_events.py
"""

